k in the pre-med track at  the same time but ultimately I decided  to go into the the business world but  just to get a sense for the audience can  I just take a quick poll who are  undergrads in the audience  and Business School students very nice  and members of the community faculty oh  great okay so we have an excellent and  diverse audience and certainly I'm  looking forward to a good dialogue  because I think people are very split on  a lot of these issues so a couple things  I'll say at the start  professor Schatz mentioned obviously I  wrote this op-ed about goldman sachs but  everything I say today I very much  believe is true to the industry more  broadly you know I don't think Goldman  definitely is a smart firm and an  excellent firm of what it does but I  don't think it's practices are very  different in a lot of ways to the  industry more broadly so you know when I  when I wrote the book and I wrote the  op-ed I was using my career there as as  a prism to the what I think has become  conflicted in the industry more  generally this the second thing I would  say before I start is I think a lot of  people would want to classify my views  or this talk as in the Occupy Wall  Street camp or in you know a different  type of camp what I would say is I'm  very much a capitalist I just have the  view that free markets imply that the  playing field is fair and there aren't  lots of conflicts of interest and a big  thesis of my book and what I was trying  to get across in the op-ed is the fact  that capitalism at least in the  financial industry as it's going on now  I no longer think is on an even playing  field  I think advantages are stacked in favor  of banks and against their investors and  clients in many respects and I think  there's maximum incentive for the system  not to change and I'll get into this a  little bit more later on but let me tell  you the three things I'm going to  discuss today the first is has anything  really changed in the industry certainly  when I wrote the op-ed a lot of people  said well  things have always been this way or Wall  Street has always operated in a certain  sense so I'm going to tell you how at  least since I was sitting where you were  15 years ago in my opinion things have  changed the second thing I'm going to  tell you is with all these changes how  do banks exploit conflicts of interest  to make money in a way that I view as  unfair at best and potentially very  systemically dangerous to society at  worst and the third point will be a  little bit about what needs to change in  order to fix this and why hasn't enough  changed and you know just on that point  I would ask how many people in this room  have heard of the dodd-frank Act how  many people think the dodd-frank Act has  been implemented so so four and a half  years after the crisis and two and a  half years since dodd-frank was passed  less than one-third of dodd-frank has  been implemented and more than  three-quarters of the deadlines have  been missed and four hundred million  dollars has been spent lobbying against  it so I want to touch a little bit at  the end about this perception that  conditions have changed and and really  try to show you guys why they haven't  changed as much as I think Obama or  Romney or any number of politicians  would want people to think so starting  with the the point of what has changed  so I think absolutely they've always  been practices on Wall Street  that are not great and that are dubious  but what I would say the simplest way to  mark what has changed is look how banks  make their money so when I joined the  firm in 2000 or let's just say when I  joined Wall Street at least half the  money was being made in the original  things that made finance get called  finance Wall Street financed things that  helped companies raise money they  advised companies on merging they acted  as an advisor in all sorts of capacities  so when I joined about 60% of gold  sexist revenue was in the banking side  of the business where certainly there  are always going to be conflicts but  there are less conflicts than in the  trading side of the business so fast  forward to 2007 close to 80% of the  money is being made in the trading  business which you know is a very marked  change which I don't think people can  debate as being a you know non factual  but what that kind of revenue change  does is it leads to behavioral changes  and cultural changes so what occurred  that allowed this revenue shift to  change three main things occurred right  around the time when I was joining Wall  Street the first thing in 1999 there was  a thing called the glass-steagall Act  which separated commercial banks from  investment banks it was repealed in 2000  there was a thing called the Commodity  Futures Modernization Act which  essentially took complex securities  called derivatives and deregulated them  so allowed the trading of derivatives to  move off exchanges basically into the  dock and the third thing that happened  was a thing called the net capital rule  was overturned and what that did is it  allowed banks to increase their leverage  from now leverage for those who don't  know is basically if you have $1 you can  effectively lever yourself up by taking  bets 10 or 20 or 30 times the amount  with the $1 you have by borrowing extra  money so from the early 2000s to the  mid-2000s banks took their leverage from  you know five six seven to one up to  thirty five to one so with these three  things which were all regulatory in  nature and which were all ultimately  lobbied for by the banking industry it  became very clear that as a professor  said the old model of long-term greedy  which their long-term greedy was a  modder that Goldman Sachs had for many  years which essentially said  we do right by clients over the long  term and maybe we turn away business in  the short term that's going to affect  our reputation ultimately they'll keep  coming back to us and we'll have