tion or to bet  with the firm's own money and no one is  telling them that there's anything wrong  with that  it ultimately filters up and down an  organization and this cultural shift  occurred everywhere on Wall Street  during that period looking you know just  a few examples too to show this this  mindset shift just in the last year or  so I mean you can count down the what  I'd call scandals that show this type of  swing for the fences behavior you know  you had MF Global which was a futures  brokerage in Chicago where the head of  the firm started taking proprietary bets  on the sovereign crisis in Europe and  ultimately the firm went bankrupt  there was a scandalous situation where  there were potentially using customer  money to fund these bad bets and no one  no one has been prosecuted for that  secondly in the last few months we've  all heard about the LIBOR rigging  scandal LIBOR is an interest rate that  affects hundreds of trillions of dollars  of assets around the world and this is  this is I would say a an example of this  incentive where traders were  collaborating with each other to set an  interest rate because they knew they'd  make more money for the firm you look at  the Facebook IPO there we can argue  endlessly was a Facebook's fault was it  Morgan Stanley's fault was it the  investors fault but certainly a  manifestation of this you know let's  swing for the fences try to get as much  out of this deal as possible so let me  let me go into point two which is a  little bit trying to explain how banks  use make this money using a little bit  of analogy which I use in my book if you  think if you compare Wall Street today  which seventy five percent of revenues  come from trading to a real casino so  not it not everyone likes gambling but  whether we like gambling or don't like  gambling  when you walk into a casino you can have  a lot of faith that the rules are not  going to change during the game and that  there are cameras all over the floor and  that there is a regulatory body that for  the most part is keeping an eye on  things the other thing I would say is  the casino or the house is not allowed  to take bets based on what everyone in  the casino is doing the casino is a what  I'd call an objective counterparty so  let's let's just run through this  analogy a little bit if you think about  an investment bank they are dealing  everyone cards and let's say at the  table is sitting a pension fund a hedge  fund a mutual fund you know hundreds of  billions of dollars of investors are  sitting at this table that bank or the  dealer can see what all those people are  doing and can then go out and use it  information to place their own bets now  you would expect someone not to lose  very often if you can see everyone's  cards and this is very much borne out in  an example we see every quarter where  banks have to release their results on  their trading books for the whole  quarter  there are many quarters where a bank  will literally make a profit 100% of the  time so that that's like batting a  thousand where you know if you go to the  plate you would not expect someone to  hit every time so you got to think to  yourself I'm sure most people in this  room invest in the market if you make  money fifty point one percent of the  time you're probably outperforming many  many people in the market it's very hard  to understand how a trading book can  make money 100 percent of the time  unless you have some kind of asymmetric  information advantage and and that is  you know where I would come back to the  analogy of you're essentially seeing  what everyone else is doing so you can  bet a lot smarter with your own money  second part of the analogy when I  mentioned the cameras what happened with  the deregulation of derivatives is the  gambling can be taken to a backroom  where there are no cameras and no one is  trekking who is losing a lot of money  and who is making a lot of money and  this is the example of Leh