ecause that person walked into the  casino they  you are responsible for their own  actions and I think this is where a big  misunderstanding comes in and you know  I'm sure many of you remember in 2010  there was a big SEC lawsuit against  Goldman Sachs and there was a big  hearing in front of Carl Levin where a  lot of swear words we used some TV the  one particular word beginning with an  Shi tty was used I think 20 times  because of how Bank has spoke about  these deals you know the big argument  for why it was okay to sell someone  something without full disclosure or  without fully telling them your  intentions was this idea that they're a  big boy and everyone's a big boy if  they've if they've come to play they  deserve what's coming to them even if  there is a sense of deception or a sense  of misleading them now I think a lot  about the word fiduciary duty and you  know this is a word that I think is  thrown around a lot but the central  meaning of it is when someone is coming  to you for advice if you are bound by a  fiduciary obligation you owe that client  or that person the duty of telling them  what is in their interest and what is  not in their interest and I think over  the time I was in Wall Street there has  it is morphed into the sense that a  fiduciary duty is not owed to anyone the  big problem with that is I would argue  many many clients with hundreds of  billions of dollars of assets don't  understand it and you know an example  I'll give you two examples that  ultimately led to me writing the op-ed  and and you know I'd like all of you to  think about just as an ethical dilemma  especially since we live in a time today  where the idea of ethics versus legal  are very different things if something  is technically legal and it's not going  to get you sent to jail a lot of people  will do those things so the example I  would give you to think about is that we  recently had a terrible storm hurricane  sandy everyone gives donation  to say the R