 good  things are gone on Wall Street but there  are a lot of conflicts of interest and  only if the public holds politicians  feet to the fire will things change so  my hope is that and really a question  was you know how can business school  students and college students change  things the one is speak to your  politicians hold them accountable fools  fill yourself in on the issues and when  you do go work in finance act within  your own ethical compass and you know  one by one I think people can change  things but I do think politicians  ultimately probably hard with the  largest amount of blame in this problem  hi Greg this is a great segue to my next  question what are your plans in the  future as far as your career I think  you'd be great in Congress or working  for the SEC or the Department of Justice  with your background and values and I'm  sure there's people in this room who  would support you oh thank you well I've  told some people I'm becoming a DJ which  is not a very very popular thing just  just as a joke but no I mean in the in  the short term my plan is to try to  bring awareness to I'd say the the most  short term crisis which is the fact that  dodd-frank is dying what I call a very  slow death by a thousand cuts  and you know I'm sure everyone tuned in  to the presidential election not one  candidate talks about financial reform  doesn't matter if you're a Democrat or  Republican there's literally I would say  one person running for Senate who even  mentioned it was Elizabeth Warren who  you know we can agree or disagree about  the I would say whether her views are  accurate or not but what I do commend  her for is that she is talking about it  which I think it would affect people's  lives politicians should talk about it  so I want to be part through giving  talks like this to bring a little bit of  awareness to what I view as a serious  problem and then longer term I would  like to be helpful to Congress I would  like to be helpful to the SEC I have  been I've been in touch with with people  in Congress and I hope to be helpful to  them  and in terms of my longer-term plans I I  haven't figured it out yet I think a lot  of people said well you burnt your  bridges on Wall Street you're never  going to work there again but I think  the point I make to people is like  that's not what I'm hearing from the  public in terms of this idea of  returning to a fiduciary standard  because as I said the big players in the  market are not hedge funds hedge funds  are rich investors account for less than  5% of the markets the big players are  teachers pension funds and sovereign  wealth funds and mutual funds who hold  people's 401ks and donations and  retirement savings so I do think  ultimately I would like to be part of  the solution in terms of bringing a  greater fiduciary standard back to  markets I'm sorry yeah earlier in your  talk you mentioned Merrill Lynch and  Lehman Brothers and Bear Stearns as  being three firms that were what over  leveraged and operating in the dark and  they kind of disappeared could you could  you give us your thoughts on why Lehman  was allowed to kind of burn up and the  other two were so-called rescued it's a  it's a very current very controversial  topic which I think conspiracy theorists  will talk about for a long time but I  think you know I was I was sitting on  the trading floor at the time that  happened and you know at when Lehman  Brothers was allowed to fail you know  any of any record checkers or fact  checkers should go back and look at the  editorials the following day both  liberal periodicals and conservative  periodicals praised Hank Paulson for  drawing a line in the sand and saying  you know what like if you take  irresponsible risk and your leaders  aren't willing to you know fix the  problem we'll show the right amount of  transparency you are going to be allowed  to die and ultimately when the  government reverse course on AIG and on  multiple other things I think it just  showed a tremendous amount of  uncertainty to the markets and everyone  panicked and they led to more problems  so looking back on that I I think it was  a mismanagement in that what markets  like and anyone invests in stocks will  know markets like consistency they like  to have foreseeability into what's going  to happen in the future and the fact  that every day was a guessing game of  who is going to be allowed to live and  who was going to be allowed to die it  was a very dangerous thing so I think  perhaps everyone should have been saved  or everyone should have been allowed to  die and I you know I think everyone  would have been scared to see what  happened but and it's an extreme view  but ultimately I think history will look  back on it and you know the crisis  probably would have been worse if Lehman  or its I'd say it's very hard to say and  I would say you need a crystal ball in  order to tell that's uh first of all  thank you for speaking out I think on  Wall Street a lot of paychecks are  dependent on not speaking out so I  wanted to thank you for that there is  precedent for prosecuting people for  securities fraud like in the 1980s  during the kings Savings and Loan  scandal just wanted your thoughts about  how or why there have been zero criminal  prosecutions of any Wall Street  executives yeah it's a it's an excellent  question and and I think a question  everyone in America asks and I'll tell  you the reason is quite simple it's that  the laws are not I would say on the  level that allows criminal prosecutions  to take place very easily and this is  what Wall Street wants right now you  need to prove criminal intent to defraud  people and now you look at any number  number of examples you look at the MF  Global example which is a particularly  egregious one where Jon Corzine who was  the former head of Goldman and ended up  being a senator and a Governor of New  Jersey essentially a billion dollars of  money just disappeared and the reason or  at least the reason Congress will tell  the public that no one goes to jail even  gets prosecuted is because in order to  prove intent that they were trying to  defraud people is a very hard thing to  do so what I would argue and and as I've  said I'm not I'm not I wouldn't classify  myself as a pro  regulation person but I think any  thinking person realizes that if you're  if everyone is driving race cause and  there are zero speed limits and everyone  is just mandated to self-regulate and to  not crash into people ultimately you're  going to get bad actors that crash into  other people so I think I think the laws  need to change and the laws need to be  more strict and you know the hope was  the momentum come out of the crisis  would be used to change those laws but  unfortunately we're we're kind of four  years in the distance things are fading  into memory and frankly that's what  banks want they want this to recede into  memory you know personally I thought the  JP Morgan example was was something that  should have been a much bigger deal  because it was very symptomatic of this  reckless we gamble and if we win we keep  the profits if we lose we don't have to  worry about it I think it was an  opportunity for Congress to change  things and you know Preet Bharara will  be at the Business School next week has  done some great work prosecuting insider  trading which frankly right now is what  I call the low-hanging fruit because  it's easy to prove a billionaire used  misinformation in order to make some  money for himself or herself the truth  is insider trading doesn't actually  affect that many people the real issue  in my mind that that the Justice  Department and lawmakers should be going  after is this systemic issue of you know  I mentioned earlier this idea of  proprietary trading no longer goes on  what I would tell you is it's absolutely  going on it's just going on under what I  call a new term which is hedging or  market making we're doing this in  service of clients the truth is in my  mind and in my opinion much less is  being done in service of clients much  more is being done by using the fact  you're serving clients in order to make  yourself smarter to place your own bets  and I just think Congress needs to  become a lot smarter and fixing that  rule and if they can fix that rule I  actually think it would be more valuable  than sending two or three people to jail  because a great danger of that is then  everyone says well we've not fixed the  problem the real problem is much greater  than that is much more systemic too  mind yes you mentioned that mutual funds  are among the major players in the  market um do these systemic factors that  you've mentioned have a significant  impact I will call mainstream mutual  funds you know that invest in equities  or fixed income as opposed to some of  the fringe players and should we as  individual investors have any concerns  in that area I absolutely and I'll give  you a couple examples sure people  remember in May 2010 there was a thing  called the flash crash where the market  literally dropped something like 12% in  one second and no one could explain what  happened and you know I think people who  had researched the issue speak about a  thing called high-frequency trading  which Wall Street firms have computers  that they get to the stock a millisecond  before anyone else and this causes a lot  of volatility so I would argue mutual  funds no matter how vanilla they are  they're still investing your money in  stocks and if the market can't be  assumed to be fair and without uneven  advantages I do think people should be  concerned and I do think your mutual  funds should also be held accountable  not to use complex products like  derivatives when they don't fully  understand them because a lot of the  time they don't thank you thank you very  much I have a question that's related to  some of the answers that you've provided  you've in I think all of your responses  you've talked about regulations that are  instituted and enforced and then you've  also talked about incentives and perhaps  disincentives for some of the behavior  that exists I'd like to understand given  the complexity of enforcing regulations  and and the process that you've advised  the audience here than the new grads and  and the future generation of finances in  Wall Street what would you recommend on  the disincentive side aside from the  sort of the legal consequences of  violating the regulation something that  that actually relates to the financial  rewards and and then in that context if  you could also  - tell us what differentiates what goes  on here in the US vs. did versus the  European market  a good question I think a lot of lot has  been raised about compensation on Wall  Street and I think you know one of the  ways a Wall Street firm will defend  paying people 10 or 20 or 30 million  dollars is that well if we don't do it  they'll just go somewhere else and  they'll do it and I think this is a  circular thing that keeps going on round  around and around and I think if firms  decided to bring incentives back in line  things would change I think the way you  do that is you have to tie compensation  and incentives to performance of your  clients and you have to tie it to a  long-run orientation where you take away  the incentive like the London whale had  to swing for the fences because they  wanted a big bonus in year one you have  to make compensation based on a five or  ten year track record where claw backs  are very strict they're actually enacted  you know yesterday I'm sure many people  saw at you know Jamie Dimon who was a  CEO of JPMorgan his compensation was cut  because of because of the London whale  incident and people might say well  that's a great victory what I would say  is it to me it's more of a band-aid it's  trying to show the public we're doing  something there needs to be a more  systemic change in incentives but the  truth is that human nature and greed is  something we as a people in my mind  cannot regulate and and I think you know  if one had to fault some of the protests  that took place around Wall Street you  know what I think is effective is  protest something very simple and  factual like bring derivatives onto  exchanges outlaw proprietary trading so  don't give people the ability to swing  for the fences because you take away the  products are using to do that I think to  try to tell people you need to make less  money or you know we're going to cap  your pay at X amount or Y amount doesn't  I don't think jives with American  capitalism as I see it I think people  should be at  to get rich and should be able to make  lots of money I just think they should  be required to do it in a transparent  way that doesn't endanger other people  and I think the easiest solution in my  mind is hold regulators accountable hold  politicians accountable and take away  the things that allow people to swing  for the fences by regulating the markets  more smartly I would say in terms of the  question about Europe versus the US  Europe I would say is is more like the  Wild West than in the US and the last  chapter of my book when I moved to  London I actually called the chapter the  Wild West because there's very much a  greater sense of anything-goes but I  would say the u.s. is a leader in a lot  of these products and Europe tends to  follow so if the US can get these laws  right you know the reason I think banks  don't want Congress to pass these laws  is I say well if we do it here banks  will move to Europe I guarantee you if  the u.s. changes something makes a  system more responsible there is going  to be a sense of awe betras around the  world where everyone's trying to get  somewhere else but ultimately the u.s.  is the biggest strongest market in the  world and I think others would follow if  we did things a bit better here we next  oh go ahead Greg in terms of your your  second point of the asymmetric nature of  information and the casino analogy when  we talk about deposit creation like this  going on right now like that  the last month of last year there was  220 billion of deposit creation in the  US system that's working its way into  the fractional reserves and then the  banks are obviously hedging as you  pointed out possibly not in our best  interest so in terms of that deposit  creation in December that's the largest  in history who where do you think that  money is coming from and in the casino  analogy who is that person i you know i  think i think deposit correction in a  sense comes from the fact that you look  at retail investors and how much they've  been investing in the stock market it's  since since the flash crash in 2010 it's  essentially dropped significantly so I  think there is a sense that people want  to just put their money in a bank  account and keep it safe to an extent  and I think it's a fearfulness that  comes from investing in the market now  do I believe that a big bank like a  Citigroup or JP Morgan is is necessarily  using those funds incredibly recklessly  I don't think so but are they do they  have hedging groups that claim to be  hedging the bank's portfolio but are  actually prop trading yes I do and that  was that was the example of the London  whale so I would hope the system and the  stock market becomes a little bit more  transparent where investors don't feel  the need to put their money into savings  accounts but can actually invest because  now that's what's helped the market grow  for so long you've mentioned a number of  banks that have behaved rather badly but  what are the banks that have behaved  well and have actually they been more  profitable than the banks that have  behaved badly if not then the you've got  a reversal of incentives yeah I think I  think you look at you know look at the  Canadian banks just as an example they  did not have a similar unending leverage  they didn't get into the same complex  product and they weathered the financial  crisis just fine I would say in the u.s.  you look at banks like Wells Fargo you  know that didn't have a similar I would  say ratcheting up of risk that kept that  fiduciary minded responsibility you look  at some of the more boutique asset  management places whose interests  weren't all over the place and de Neve  all sorts of conflicts and I think they  they survived a little bit better so in  retrospect I think banks like Goldman  Sachs and Morgan Stanley and frankly JP  Morgan were lucky to survive the crisis  and I think it was a domino effect that  was occurring that that the government  ultimately stepped in and saved so I  think could have happened to anyone but  the people who weathered the crisis just  fine were the ones who retained this  fiduciary duty  they weren't betting against their  clients they weren't using client  information to make themselves more  money at the time they were less  profitable but five years later I would  argue they're a lot stronger and unless  shaky ground so to speak in the case of  Canada was because of any government  regulation it was you know banks weren't  allowed to take thirty to one leverage  they weren't allowed to proprietary  trade in the same sense so in a while we  as Americans want maximum profit and  maximum capitalization like I was saying  that the speed limit topic is relevant  and if you have smart curbs that don't  allow you to make you know 50 times the  amount of money but also don't allow you  to lose 50 times the amount of money I  think it's it's just smarter and safer  for the system more broadly thank you  I wanted to say I applaud your courage  that was a great day to post on Facebook  your announcement and get feedback from  friends and family you've talked about  the ethical and legal dimensions of your  decision-making process was there a this  is your token seminarians question was  there a moral or a religious dimension  to that I you know a theme that runs  throughout my book is I am I am Jewish  and I do I do think one's upbringing has  some effect on things I want to talk  about idea of you know a charity or a  philanthropy or a university being  charged hidden fees I do think there is  a an ethical element that weighs on you  which I think stems from one's religious  upbringing and so I I do I do think it  had some impact on me and I think it  goes to this fine line between ethics  and and legal which I think there are  too many people who are willing to make  decisions that are technically legal but  not necessarily moral and I think  religion and my mind does play a part in  that  thanks for the question why don't we  take one last question they will wrap it  up basically sort of like a summation a  in order to effect change you have to  almost use the KISS principle keep it  simple stupid wouldn't couldn't you boil  down what you're talking about to two  items on a macro basis repeal the repeal  of the glass-steagall and and the  derivatives bring it back to where it  was in the Clinton era because it seemed  to have worked for 70 plus years and  secondly on a micro basis wouldn't  restrictions such as what's happened  with Morgan Stanley where the traders  now get compensated in deferred  compensation over five years wouldn't  that create the disincentives to bet the  house for next year's bonus a great  question I think EEP it simple I  completely agree with I I mean the two  major regulatory things I'm advocating  for are essentially repeal are the two  things that happened in the early 2000s  that led to a lot of the problems which  is derivatives being reregulate 'add  you know the CFTC has been sitting on  for two-and-a-half years and frankly has  not made a lot of progress achieving and  the second is the Volcker Rule which in  a sense is a little bit like many of the  things that glass-steagall stood for I  mean there's certainly people who stand  for the full repeal of glass-steagall  think they're huge political fears about  doing that but I do think some form of  breaking up extremely large banks  disallowing reckless trading activity is  noteworthy I think I think the thing  about compensation and deferring over  five years is essential but does it fix  the problem completely I don't think so  because the truth is if you swing for  the fence in your one and your bonus you  know and things don't work out you can  still leave and go to a hedge fund or go  do something else  I do think the law actually has to  become a little bit stricter because I  think greed and swinging for the fences  is always going to be around and is  impossible to start so I think you  actually need to change the root cause  which is you know if someone  Gambel's with clients money or bets  against the client that person  potentially needs to go to jail or needs  to have some disincentive that's so  great that if they're a reckless person  they're going to be two or three things  they think about before they endanger  the whole house so I think laws have to  change in addition to compensation  changing but but not not irresponsible  laws but just ones that create curves  that don't allow reckless activity I  guess your next talk is to the law  school  and I'd love to do it well thank you  very much  tomorrow the arms control Association  holds
 

